Worth stating plainly: this is an internal contradiction in the section of a document whose entire subject is internal contradictions. The page exists partly to catalogue places where beadle docs disagree with themselves, including a tool count that said 8 in one section and 21+2 in another. It shipped with one of its own. Corrected to five."}){ clientMutationId }
  b: addPullRequestReviewThreadReply(input:{pullRequestReviewThreadId:"PRRT_kwDORbZKrs6dtCdd", body:"Valid — fixed in `e174d4b`. `--bg: #16151300; --bg: #161513;` on one line, with the first value dead on arrival. Leftover from testing a transparent background before settling on the opaque one. Removed."}){ clientMutationId }
  c: addPullRequestReviewThreadReply(input:{pullRequestReviewThreadId:"PRRT_kwDORbZKrs6dtO1G", body:"Valid — fixed in `e174d4b`, and a real defect rather than cosmetic, since precedence between duplicate attributes is not defined by spec.

Cause: the mermaid renderer emits `role="graphics-document document"` on the `<svg>` itself. When I switched the embed from `<img src="data:...">` to an inline `<svg>`, I added `role="img"` and `aria-label` without checking whether the element already carried a role. Kept `role="img"` and dropped the mermaid one: `graphics-document` has thinner assistive-technology support, and this is a static rendered diagram rather than an interactive graphics document, so `img` plus a descriptive `aria-label` is the accurate semantics.

Context on why the embed changed at all — the `<img>` form could not work. Mermaid renders every label inside `<foreignObject>` (35 of them, zero `<text>` elements), and Chrome and Safari do not render `foreignObject` for an SVG loaded through `<img>`, so the diagram would have shown boxes and arrows with no text whatsoever. Verified `foreignObject` is still intact at 35 after this fix."}){ clientMutationId }
